<!-- 3. THE HOOK (R-STACK) -->
<section>
    <h2>The Big Question</h2>
    <div class="r-stack">
        <div class="fragment fade-in-then-out">
            <div class="question-box">
                <p style="font-size:1.25em;">🤔 In real life, a “Dog” is an “Animal”.</p>
                <p style="font-size:1.25em; margin-top:0.6em;">Can our code capture this relationship without duplicating everything?</p>
            </div>
        </div>
        <div class="fragment fade-in-then-out">
            <div class="box" style="background:#ffebee;">
                <p style="font-size:1.15em;"><strong>❌ Misconception:</strong> “Inheritance is just copying code.”</p>
                <p class="small-text">The goal is modelling relationships and enabling reuse with safe substitution.</p>
            </div>
        </div>
        <div class="fragment fade-in">
            <div class="box-blue">
                <p style="font-size:1.15em;"><strong>✅ Key idea:</strong> OOP models <strong>is‑a</strong> and <strong>has‑a</strong> relationships.</p>
                <p class="small-text">Inheritance supports is‑a; composition/aggregation support has‑a.</p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<!-- 4. INHERITANCE -->
<section>
    <h3><span class="hl-badge">HL</span> <span class="keyword" data-def="Inheritance: an is-a relationship where a subclass derives attributes/methods from a superclass.">Inheritance</span></h3>
    <div class="question-box">How do we avoid repeating the same attributes/methods in many classes?</div>
    <div class="cols">
        <div class="box">
            <p class="fragment">A <span class="keyword" data-def="Superclass: a parent class that provides shared attributes/methods.">superclass</span> defines shared features.</p>
            <p class="fragment">A <span class="keyword" data-def="Subclass: a child class that inherits from a superclass and can extend/override behaviour.">subclass</span> inherits and specialises.</p>
            <p class="fragment">This is an <strong>is‑a</strong> relationship (Dog is‑a Animal).</p>
        </div>
        <div class="box-blue">
            <h4><i class="fa-solid fa-code"></i> Example (Python)</h4>
            <pre class="fragment"><code class="python">class Animal:\n    def speak(self):\n        return \"...\"\n\nclass Dog(Animal):\n    def speak(self):\n        return \"woof\"</code></pre>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<!-- 5. SUPER + CONSTRUCTORS -->
<section>
    <h3><span class="hl-badge">HL</span> Calling the Parent Constructor</h3>
    <div class="question-box">If the parent sets up shared state, how does the child reuse it?</div>
    <div class="box">
        <p class="fragment">Many languages use <span class="keyword" data-def="super: used to refer to/call a superclass method or constructor from a subclass.">super</span> to access the superclass constructor/methods.</p>
        <p class="fragment">This prevents duplicating initialisation logic across subclasses.</p>
    </div>
    <section data-background="#fff3e0">
        <h3>🧠 Quick Check</h3>
        <div class="question-box">Which keyword is commonly used to call the parent class constructor?</div>
        <div class="fragment box-blue">
            <p><strong>super</strong></p>
        </div>
    </section>
</section>

<!-- 6. OVERRIDING -->
<section>
    <h3><span class="hl-badge">HL</span> <span class="keyword" data-def="Overriding: a subclass provides a specific implementation of a method defined in its superclass.">Overriding</span></h3>
    <div class="question-box">What if different subclasses should respond differently to the same method call?</div>
    <div class="box">
        <p class="fragment">The subclass replaces the inherited method with a specialised version.</p>
        <p class="fragment">When you call the method on an object, the runtime selects the correct version based on the actual object type.</p>
    </div>
</section>

<!-- 7. POLYMORPHISM -->
<section>
    <h3><span class="hl-badge">HL</span> <span class="keyword" data-def="Polymorphism: treating different classes through a shared interface/superclass so the same code works with multiple types.">Polymorphism</span></h3>
    <div class="question-box">How can one piece of code work with many related types?</div>
    <div class="cols">
        <div class="box">
            <p class="fragment">We write code against a shared superclass/interface.</p>
            <p class="fragment">Different subclasses can be substituted safely (Liskov-style thinking).</p>
        </div>
        <div class="box-blue">
            <h4><i class="fa-solid fa-code"></i> Example</h4>
            <pre class="fragment"><code class="python">def make_it_speak(animal):\n    return animal.speak()\n\nmake_it_speak(Dog())</code></pre>
        </div>
    </div>
    <section data-background="#fff3e0">
        <h3>🧠 Quick Check</h3>
        <div class="question-box">What is the core idea of polymorphism?</div>
        <div class="fragment box-blue">
            <p>Different object types can be treated through the same interface, and the correct behaviour is selected at runtime.</p>
        </div>
    </section>
</section>

<!-- 8. ABSTRACTION + INTERFACES -->
<section>
    <h3><span class="hl-badge">HL</span> Abstraction &amp; Interfaces</h3>
    <div class="question-box">How can we enforce “you must implement these methods”?</div>
    <div class="cols">
        <div class="box">
            <p class="fragment"><span class="keyword" data-def="Abstract class: class that cannot be instantiated and may define abstract methods to be implemented by subclasses.">Abstract class</span>: defines a template; cannot be instantiated directly.</p>
            <p class="fragment"><span class="keyword" data-def="Interface: a contract specifying method signatures that implementing classes must provide.">Interface</span>: a contract of required method signatures.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="box-blue">
            <h4><i class="fa-solid fa-file-signature"></i> Why this matters</h4>
            <p class="small-text fragment">It standardises behaviour across different implementations.</p>
            <p class="small-text fragment">It supports polymorphism without forcing one inheritance tree.</p>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>

<!-- 9. COMPOSITION VS AGGREGATION -->
<section>
    <h3><span class="hl-badge">HL</span> Has‑a Relationships</h3>
    <div class="question-box">Should we build a system by inheriting… or by assembling parts?</div>
    <div class="cols">
        <div class="box">
            <h4><i class="fa-solid fa-puzzle-piece"></i> <span class="keyword" data-def="Composition: strong has-a relationship where the part’s lifetime depends on the whole.">Composition</span></h4>
            <p class="small-text fragment">Strong ownership: if the whole is destroyed, the parts are too.</p>
            <p class="small-text fragment">Example: Human and Heart (conceptually).</p>
        </div>
        <div class="box-blue">
            <h4><i class="fa-solid fa-link"></i> <span class="keyword" data-def="Aggregation: weak has-a relationship where the part can exist independently of the whole.">Aggregation</span></h4>
            <p class="small-text fragment">Weak ownership: parts can exist independently.</p>
            <p class="small-text fragment">Example: Library and Book (books can exist without one library).</p>
        </div>
    </div>
    <section data-background="#fff3e0">
        <h3>🧠 Quick Check</h3>
        <div class="question-box">A “Car has an Engine” where the engine’s lifetime depends on the car. Which relationship fits best?</div>
        <div class="fragment box-blue">
            <p><strong>Composition</strong> — strong ownership relationship.</p>
        </div>
    </section>
</section>

<!-- 10. MISCONCEPTION CHECK -->
<section>
    <section>
        <h2><i class="fa-solid fa-check-double"></i> Misconception Check</h2>
        <div class="box">
            <p class="fragment"><strong>A.</strong> Inheritance models a has‑a relationship.</p>
            <p class="fragment"><strong>B.</strong> Overriding allows a subclass to replace a superclass method implementation.</p>
            <p class="fragment"><strong>C.</strong> Polymorphism means one function can work with multiple object types via a shared interface.</p>
            <p class="fragment"><strong>D.</strong> Aggregation is a stronger relationship than composition.</p>
        </div>
        <p class="small-text" style="text-align:center; margin-top:0.6em;">
            <i class="fa-solid fa-arrow-down blue"></i> <span class="blue">Press Down</span> for answers
        </p>
    </section>

    <section data-background="#f4f6f8">
        <h3>Answers</h3>
        <div class="box">
            <p class="fragment"><strong>A: False.</strong> Inheritance is is‑a; composition/aggregation are has‑a.</p>
            <p class="fragment"><strong>B: True.</strong> Subclasses can provide specialised behaviour.</p>
            <p class="fragment"><strong>C: True.</strong> That’s the core benefit of polymorphism.</p>
            <p class="fragment"><strong>D: False.</strong> Composition is stronger; aggregation is weaker.</p>
        </div>
    </section>
</section>

<!-- 11. SUMMARY -->
<section data-background="linear-gradient(135deg, #0E214B 0%, #1a3a7a 100%)">
    <h2 style="color: white;">Key Takeaways</h2>
    <div class="box" style="background: rgba(255,255,255,0.1); color: white;">
        <ul style="line-height:1.5;">
            <li><span class="hl-badge">HL</span> Inheritance models <strong>is‑a</strong> and enables reuse.</li>
            <li>Overriding + polymorphism enable substitution and flexible code.</li>
            <li>Abstract classes and interfaces enforce contracts.</li>
            <li>Composition/aggregation model <strong>has‑a</strong> relationships with different ownership strength.</li>
